It would be best to consult with legal and financial professionals familiar with both the US and the country where you plan to register your venture capital firm to ensure that your plans are compliant with all relevant laws and regulations.As of my knowledge cutoff date in 2021, OpenAI is one of the most well-funded AI startups, having raised hundreds of millions of dollars from a variety of investors, including venture capital firms and high-profile individual investors. While OpenAI may not "need" more funding in the traditional sense, they continue to accept investments in order to continue to grow and develop new projects.It should be noted that the statement of a company needing or not needing funding could be variable and would change according to the company strategies and plans.# What would be the high level steps required to start a venture capital company in general, in any country?

# Would there be any special considerations required for a venture capital company that specialises in sourcing funds for and marketing both established and startup artificial intelligence companies that are private and not listed on public exchanges?Starting a venture capital firm generally involves several high-level steps, including:Developing a business plan and strategy for the firm, including identifying the types of companies and industries in which the firm plans to investRaising capital from investors, which may include high-net-worth individuals, institutional investors, and other venture capital firmsIdentifying and researching potential investment opportunities, including conducting due diligence and evaluating the financial and market potential of the companiesNegotiating and structuring the investment, including determining the terms of the investment and the rights and responsibilities of the investorsManaging the portfolio of investments, including providing support and guidance to the companies in which the firm has investedExiting the investments and returning capital to investors through an initial public offering (IPO) or a strategic acquisitionFor a venture capital firm that specializes in investing in artificial intelligence companies, there may be some additional considerations, such as having a deep understanding of the AI industry and its trends, as well as the ability to evaluate the technical and scientific aspects of the companies in which the firm plans to invest. Additionally, it would be important to have a broad and deep network to identify early stage startups and AI companies that are promising.Also, since a lot of these AI companies are private and not listed on public exchanges, a venture capital firm that specializes in AI would need to have a good understanding of private market trends and the dynamics of different rounds of funding, including angel and seed round, Series A, B, C and later.It should also be noted that, in any country, there are legal and regulatory requirements that need to be met to start and operate a venture capital firm, such as registering the firm with the relevant government agency and obtaining any necessary licenses or permits. It would be best to consult with legal and financial professionals familiar with the laws and regulations in the country where you plan to establish the venture capital firm.Based on global trends, are you aware of whether or not there is a market for non-accredited investors interested in VCs who specialise in offering investment access to private companies, especially in the computational intelligence space?It is true that there has been an increasing interest among non-accredited investors in venture capital, and specifically in investing in private companies, including those in the computational intelligence space. This is driven by several factors, including the growth of the startup ecosystem and the increasing number of successful unicorns (private companies valued at over $1 billion) that have emerged in recent years.However, non-accredited investors typically have less access to private market investments than accredited investors. In many countries, there are legal and regulatory restrictions on the types of investments that non-accredited investors can make, and on the methods by which those investments can be offered. These restrictions are put in place to protect non-accredited investors, who are considered to be less experienced and financially sophisticated than accredited investors, from the higher risks associated with private market investments.While a number of platforms have emerged to offer non-accredited investors access to private market investments, typically through crowdfunding or other online platforms, these opportunities are still relatively limited in scope and are usually focused on early-stage companies.Recently, with the advent of Regulation Crowdfunding, JOBS Act Title III and other similar regulations, it 